            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="BOT01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename/>
                        <surname>Botwright</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000"/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Probably Mr Botwright, Upper Greeen-Street, Bennet's Row, Blackfriars
                            (Morning Chronicle, 24 February 1815), who seems to keep a Lodging
                            House, but it may also be a safe house for debtors. The connection with
                            Atcheson also suggests that he has some connection with debtors and the
                            law. Botwright appears between 1813 and 1823 only , though Godwin writes
                            him one more letter in 1826. He first appears through <persName
                                key="KEN01">James Kenney</persName>, but it is difficult to
                            establish much more through context as he is often alone (mostly calling
                            on Godwin or having tea with him). His peak years of contact are
                            1817-20. Godwin sees him at <persName key="HAZ01">Hazlitt's</persName>
                            lectures in 1818, also quite often in conjunction with the <persName
                                key="KEN01">Kenneys</persName>.</p>
                    </note>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
